1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,798
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,798 --> 00:00:09,923
This is chapter twenty-four, the start of the concurrency section: threading fundamentals.

3
00:00:10,373 --> 00:00:14,607
Almost nobody writes new Thread in application code any more.

4
00:00:14,607 --> 00:00:17,453
Executors and virtual threads sit on top.

5
00:00:17,453 --> 00:00:19,397
But every abstraction leaks.

6
00:00:19,397 --> 00:00:28,421
Thread dumps show states, interruption flows through pool APIs, and a thread that dies silently takes your background job with it.

7
00:00:29,321 --> 00:00:34,388
A process owns memory, and a thread is one path of execution through it.

8
00:00:34,388 --> 00:00:40,371
Each thread has its own stack, so local variables are private and need no protection.

9
00:00:40,821 --> 00:00:43,215
But the heap is shared by every thread.

10
00:00:43,215 --> 00:00:45,301
That's where all the danger lives.

11
00:00:45,751 --> 00:00:47,352
Why have threads at all?

12
00:00:47,352 --> 00:00:58,889
Sending five hundred orders to vendors at two hundred milliseconds each takes a hundred seconds one by one, and the CPU sits idle the whole time, waiting on network sockets.

13
00:00:58,889 --> 00:01:03,223
A thread lets the program do something else while one path waits.

14
00:01:04,123 --> 00:01:06,381
A thread moves through six states.

15
00:01:06,381 --> 00:01:08,241
NEW, before start is called.

16
00:01:08,691 --> 00:01:11,555
RUNNABLE means running or ready to run.

17
00:01:11,555 --> 00:01:17,651
But it also covers a thread blocked in operating-system I/O, like reading a socket.

18
00:01:18,101 --> 00:01:21,130
BLOCKED means waiting to enter a synchronized block.

19
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:27,745
WAITING and TIMED_WAITING mean waiting for another thread, through wait, join, park or sleep.

20
00:01:28,195 --> 00:01:31,587
And TERMINATED, once run has returned or thrown.

21
00:01:32,037 --> 00:01:35,995
That second row surprises people reading thread dumps.

22
00:01:35,995 --> 00:01:43,105
A service doing nothing but waiting on two hundred HTTP calls shows two hundred RUNNABLE threads.

23
00:01:43,105 --> 00:01:46,843
BLOCKED only ever means contending for a Java lock.

24
00:01:47,743 --> 00:01:50,916
Since Java 21, create threads with a builder.

25
00:01:50,916 --> 00:01:53,877
Give it a name, then start it with a task.

26
00:01:53,877 --> 00:01:56,980
join with a duration waits, but not forever.

27
00:01:57,430 --> 00:02:00,608
A classic bug: calling run instead of start.

28
00:02:00,608 --> 00:02:05,736
run is just an ordinary method, so the task runs on the current thread.

29
00:02:05,736 --> 00:02:10,358
No concurrency, and no error, which is what makes it a real bug.

30
00:02:10,808 --> 00:02:14,347
Prefer passing a Runnable over extending Thread.

31
00:02:14,347 --> 00:02:18,771
And Thread dot stop, suspend and resume throw since Java 20.

32
00:02:18,771 --> 00:02:25,481
stop threw an exception at an arbitrary point, releasing every lock with data half-updated.

33
00:02:25,481 --> 00:02:26,808
It was never safe.

34
00:02:27,708 --> 00:02:29,705
There's no way to kill a thread.

35
00:02:29,705 --> 00:02:32,076
You ask it to stop, and it cooperates.

36
00:02:32,076 --> 00:02:34,447
You call interrupt, which sets a flag.

37
00:02:34,447 --> 00:02:36,007
This worker checks twice.

38
00:02:36,007 --> 00:02:39,439
The loop condition catches interruption between orders.

39
00:02:39,439 --> 00:02:44,743
And take throws InterruptedException if it's interrupted while waiting for the queue.

40
00:02:44,743 --> 00:02:50,359
A worker that does only one of these either ignores interruption while it waits, or spins.

41
00:02:50,359 --> 00:02:53,479
Then shut down with interrupt, and a bounded join.

42
00:02:54,379 --> 00:03:01,285
This is the most common concurrency bug in enterprise Java, usually written by an IDE quick-fix.

43
00:03:01,285 --> 00:03:04,235
Catch InterruptedException and ignore it.

44
00:03:04,685 --> 00:03:07,572
The fix: propagate it, which is preferred.

45
00:03:07,572 --> 00:03:14,173
Or, if you can't, restore the flag by calling interrupt on the current thread, and stop working.

46
00:03:14,173 --> 00:03:17,335
Restoring without stopping is only half a fix.

47
00:03:17,785 --> 00:03:19,274
Here's why it matters.

48
00:03:19,274 --> 00:03:24,754
When a blocking method throws InterruptedException, it clears the interrupt flag.

49
00:03:24,754 --> 00:03:30,979
Swallow it and all evidence of the cancellation request is gone, so every caller carries on.

50
00:03:30,979 --> 00:03:35,919
A shutdown that should take milliseconds hangs until something times out.

51
00:03:36,819 --> 00:03:40,867
Interruption is a single boolean per thread, plus a convention.

52
00:03:40,867 --> 00:03:42,088
You call interrupt.

53
00:03:42,538 --> 00:03:52,031
If the thread is blocked in an interruptible method, like sleep, wait, join or take, the flag is cleared and InterruptedException is thrown.

54
00:03:52,481 --> 00:03:57,644
If it's running ordinary code, only the flag is set, and the thread keeps going.

55
00:03:58,094 --> 00:04:03,987
So if your loop never checks the flag and never blocks, interrupt does nothing at all.

56
00:04:03,987 --> 00:04:06,179
The JVM can't stop running code.

57
00:04:06,629 --> 00:04:07,480
Three traps.

58
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:14,213
The static Thread dot interrupted reads and clears the flag, while isInterrupted just reads it.

59
00:04:14,213 --> 00:04:20,450
Blocking socket reads mostly ignore interruption, so to cancel one you close the socket.

60
00:04:20,450 --> 00:04:22,718
And sleep never releases a lock.

61
00:04:22,718 --> 00:04:23,781
Only wait does.

62
00:04:24,681 --> 00:04:28,150
An uncaught exception terminates only that thread.

63
00:04:28,150 --> 00:04:35,988
The default handler prints to standard error, which in a containerised service often goes nowhere anyone watches.

64
00:04:35,988 --> 00:04:39,387
Your worker is dead and monitoring shows nothing.

65
00:04:39,387 --> 00:04:45,631
Set an uncaught exception handler on each worker, plus a JVM-wide default as a safety net.

66
00:04:46,081 --> 00:04:47,454
And daemon threads.

67
00:04:47,454 --> 00:04:55,259
The JVM exits when the last non-daemon thread ends, and daemons are killed abruptly, with no finally blocks.

68
00:04:55,259 --> 00:05:01,547
Use them for disposable work, like cache eviction, never for anything that writes data.

69
00:05:02,447 --> 00:05:05,333
Each platform thread is a real OS thread.

70
00:05:05,333 --> 00:05:12,581
It reserves around a megabyte of stack, so ten thousand threads reserve ten gigabytes of address space.

71
00:05:13,031 --> 00:05:18,385
Creating one takes tens to hundreds of microseconds, enough to notice per request.

72
00:05:18,835 --> 00:05:24,489
And context switches go through the operating system, and pollute the CPU caches.

73
00:05:24,939 --> 00:05:30,261
That arithmetic is why thread pools exist, and why virtual threads were built.

74
00:05:30,261 --> 00:05:33,263
One more operational tip: name every thread.

75
00:05:33,263 --> 00:05:39,677
A thread dump saying dispatch seven is blocked, owned by dispatch three, tells you everything.

76
00:05:39,677 --> 00:05:41,792
Thread seven tells you nothing.

77
00:05:42,692 --> 00:05:43,945
Production rules.

78
00:05:43,945 --> 00:05:47,631
Name every thread, including every pool's factory.

79
00:05:47,631 --> 00:05:49,989
Set uncaught exception handlers.

80
00:05:49,989 --> 00:05:52,275
Propagate InterruptedException.

81
00:05:52,275 --> 00:05:54,781
Check isInterrupted in long loops.

82
00:05:54,781 --> 00:05:57,361
Use a bounded join during shutdown.

83
00:05:57,361 --> 00:06:01,636
And prefer executors and virtual threads over raw threads.

84
00:06:02,086 --> 00:06:04,703
Never call run when you meant start.

85
00:06:04,703 --> 00:06:07,248
Never swallow InterruptedException.

86
00:06:07,248 --> 00:06:09,719
Never use stop, suspend or resume.

87
00:06:09,719 --> 00:06:13,063
Never make a thread that writes data a daemon.

88
00:06:13,063 --> 00:06:16,189
Never create a platform thread per request.

89
00:06:16,189 --> 00:06:18,879
And never use sleep for coordination.

90
00:06:18,879 --> 00:06:20,987
Use join, a latch or a queue.

91
00:06:21,887 --> 00:06:22,512
To recap.

92
00:06:22,512 --> 00:06:26,610
Each thread has its own stack, and they all share the heap.

93
00:06:26,610 --> 00:06:32,443
RUNNABLE includes threads blocked in I/O, and BLOCKED only means waiting for a lock.

94
00:06:32,443 --> 00:06:37,652
Interruption is a request, so propagate it or restore it, never swallow it.

95
00:06:37,652 --> 00:06:41,471
And uncaught exceptions vanish unless you set handlers.

96
00:06:41,921 --> 00:06:47,054
Next is chapter twenty-five, shared state, where two threads touch the same data.

97
00:06:47,054 --> 00:06:48,321
Thanks for watching.
